  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 347 AM CDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nThis morning, an area of showers and thunderstorms was ongoing\nin far southwest North Dakota along the state border. The main\nconcern with this area of convection would be isolated strong\nwinds, as the very warm and dry air mass is supportive of heat\nbursts. Elsewhere, fog has been developing across parts of the\nJames River Valley and up through much of north central North\nDakota, and will continue to monitor in case any additional\nproducts are needed for this. High-res guidance has not done\nwell already catching on to the reduced visibilities, but would\nexpect at least patchy fog to persist into mid morning. \n\nAnother hot and mostly dry day is in the forecast today, as the\nupper ridge axis continues to extend over the north central\nCONUS with a low-level thermal ridge aiding in bringing well\nabove average temperatures to the state. Forecast highs range\nfrom the lower 90s along the International Border to the 100 to\n105 range across the southwest and south central. A Heat\nAdvisory remains in effect for most of the forecast area, with\nthe exception of the northern two tiers of counties. \n\nOther forecast concerns today include a low chance for a few\nshowers and thunderstorms today in the Turtle Mountains area as\na weak surface low tracks along the International Border, and\npatchy near-surface smoke across the southwest and south\ncentral. HRRR smoke model hasn't been performing the best the\nlast day or two but is very confident in a plume of thick smoke\naccompanying the aforementioned surface low and associated\nfrontal boundary, so wanted to add it into the gridded forecast.\nWe also continue to mention near-critical fire weather\nconditions across most of western and parts of central North\nDakota, with a northwest breeze today and minimum relative\nhumidity in the lower to mid teens for areas south and west of\nthe Missouri River. \n\nTemperatures \"cool down\" a few degrees for the first half of the\nwork week, but forecast highs are still in the 90s to around 100\ndegrees F. Chances for widespread precipitation are low Monday\nand Tuesday, although can't rule out a few isolated showers and\nthunderstorms associated with embedded impulses passing over \nthe ridge axis. More substantial blended PoPs emerge on \nWednesday as ensemble guidance is more confident on a stronger \nwave moving across the Dakotas. Temperatures to end the week \ncool down a bit further into the upper 80s to upper 90s, \nalthough latest NBM percentiles show potential for warming up \nslightly for next weekend. NSSL machine learning is currently \nfavoring Wednesday and Thursday for the highest likelihood of \nsevere thunderstorms, but any day for the next week will have \nsome potential for any storms that can develop to be at least \nstrong. There finally starts to be a signal for closer to normal\ntemperatures as we get past the first few days of August.",
